title: 'fix(litep2p): stop tracking disconnected non-reserved peers in the peerset'

doc:
- audience:
  - Node Dev
  - Node Operator
  description: |-
    The `Peerset` of each litep2p notification protocol kept a `PeerState::Disconnected` entry for
    every peer it had ever seen, so the set of tracked peers grew for the lifetime of the node. The
    dominant source was inbound validation: a peer whose inbound substream was rejected — because
    there was no free inbound slot or because the protocol runs in reserved-only mode — left a
    permanent entry behind, one per peer per notification protocol.

    `PeerState::Disconnected` carries no information for a non-reserved peer: it is not counted
    towards the inbound/outbound slots and it is not part of the `ignore` set used to pick outbound
    candidates from the `Peerstore`, which makes it indistinguishable from the peer being absent.
    Those entries are now dropped on each slot allocation, so the tracked peers are bounded by the
    reserved set plus the peers that actually have an open, opening or closing substream. Reserved
    peers keep their entry, as that is what the `Peerset` uses to decide which reserved peers to
    dial.

    Connectivity, slot accounting and reputation reporting are unchanged. The
    `substrate_sub_libp2p_peerset_num_state{state="disconnected"}` gauge changes meaning as a
    result: instead of counting every peer ever seen, it now reports the reserved peers the
    protocol is currently not connected to.

crates:
- name: sc-network
  bump: patch
